Scott defines \(\Gamma\) by \(\Gamma=\bigcup_i(\{i\}\times\Gamma)\cup\{\star\}\) and \(L=\{\Gamma\}\cup\{\{i\}\times X\mid i\in\mathbf{N},\,X\in L\}\), and asks to show that \(L\) is effectively given, that the elements of \(|L|\) are the finite and infinite sequences of natural numbers, what connects the binary system \(B\) with \(L\), that the combinators of LUCID are computable maps of type \((L\to T)\to(L\to T)\) or \((L\to T)\times(L\to T)\to(L\to T)\) for the three-point truth domain \(T\) of Example 1.2, and that LUCID programs therefore define computable maps. `Gamma` is the sum of finite lists and streams, and `Gamma_cases` puts every token at `star` or at a `cons`, which is that equation for the terminal solution. `nbhd` sends the empty list to all of \(\Gamma\) and a cons cell to the `consSet` of the tail neighbourhood, and `L_nestedOrDisjoint` proves the nested-or-disjoint law by induction on addresses, with unequal heads giving an empty intersection of cons-sets. `Lpres` enumerates by `Lenum`, decides inclusion by the prefix characteristic (`Lenum_subset_iff`, `isPrefixChar`), decides consistency by the arithmetic disjunction of the two prefix bits (`Lenum_cons_iff`, `LenumConsChar`), and intersects by selecting the more specific code when the addresses are nested (`LenumInter`, `LenumInter_spec`); `L_isEffectivelyGiven` is that presentation. `toElement` sends a finite list to the principal filter of its neighbourhood and a stream to `streamElement`, the filter of neighbourhoods containing a finite prefix; `toElement_injective` separates the finite case from the infinite case by `HasMin` (`principal_hasMin` against `streamElement_not_hasMin`), and `toElement_surjective` uses a least neighbourhood when one exists and otherwise rebuilds the stream by `buildData` with classical choice, so `toElement_bijective` is the identification of elements with sequences. The connection with \(B\) that is proved is the digit embedding `embStr`: `embStr_prefix_iff` reflects prefixes, `cone_subset_cone_iff_nbhd_embStr` matches cone inclusion in \(B\) with neighbourhood inclusion in \(L\), and `sigmaBot_le_iff_toElement_inl_embStr` matches the order of finite elements; there is no theorem embedding the infinite binary sequences. `notFn` swaps the neighbourhoods zero and one and fixes the master, and `andFn` returns the second neighbourhood when the first is zero, the neighbourhood one when the first is one, and the master otherwise, reading zero as true; `notT_isComputable` and `andT_isComputable` reduce those relations to decidable inclusion in `Tpres` after `notCode` and `andCode`. `postcompose_isComputable` and `pointwiseBin_isComputable` lift a computable map by evaluation, composition, and currying, and `notT_lifted_isComputable` and `andT_lifted_isComputable` inhabit the two requested types. `deMorganT_isComputable` is the one composite \(\neg(\neg f\land\neg g)\). The stream combinators first, next, and followed-by are not defined. The gloss of `Exercise724` says `example_724` is effectivity of \(L\), injectivity of the digit map, and the sequence bijection, but the theorem is effectivity of \(L\), computability of `postcompose notT` and of `pointwiseBin andT`, and `toElement_bijective`; `bitToNat_injective` is not a conjunct. **Theorem:** partial — effectivity, the sequence bijection, the finite embedding of \(B\), and the two lifted truth-value combinators are proved, while infinite binary sequences are not embedded and the temporal LUCID combinators are absent. **Proof:** partial — the cone presentation, the filter bijection, and the lifting of negation and left-strict conjunction follow the geometry of \(L\) and the closure of computable maps, but one De Morgan term is not an interpretation of an arbitrary LUCID program.
